<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Bosch has two code sets: the ones in your manual, and the ones a tech reads.</h2>

    <p><strong>Bosch's <span class="hl">consumer self-help codes</span> — the E-numbers in the teens and twenties (E14, E15, E16, E17, E18, E22, E24, E25) — are the ones documented for owners. The <span class="hl">low E0-series (E01–E09)</span> are Bosch service/technician codes that aren't in your booklet. The most common by far is <span class="code">E15</span> (a base-pan leak). And two people constantly mix up: <span class="code">E24</span> is a blocked drain path/filter, <span class="code">E25</span> is the drain pump or its cover — different parts. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>This page owns the code lookup. The deep fixes for the drain codes and the leak code live on their own pages so we're not repeating ourselves: the full drain walkthrough (E22/E23/E24/E25) is on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/">Bosch dishwasher not draining</a> page, and <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-e15-error/">Bosch E15</a> has its own dedicated guide. Here you get the complete table, the consumer-vs-service split, and which code sends you where.</p>

    <p>Everything here applies to <strong>Thermador</strong> too (same BSH platform). Parent reference: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">all dishwasher error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/bosch-dishwasher-repair/">Bosch dishwasher repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Consumer codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The E-codes documented for owners (E14–E25).</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Where to start / who fixes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>E15</strong></td><td><strong>AquaStop leak — water in the base pan</strong></td><td>Drain the pan + fix the leak source → <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-e15-error/">E15 guide</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>E14</td><td>Flow meter (Reed switch) fault</td><td>Fill/flow sensing (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E16</td><td>Unwanted water inflow (inlet not closing)</td><td>Inlet valve (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E17</td><td>Too much / uncontrolled inflow</td><td>Check hose kinks, water supply; inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E18</td><td>Water not drawn in (level too low)</td><td>Open tap fully, clear inlet filters, low pressure (~10 L/min)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E22</td><td>Filter blocked / residual water in sump</td><td>Clean filter cylinder → often $89 dx only</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>E24</strong></td><td><strong>Drain route/filter blocked (not draining)</strong></td><td>Filter, drain hose, siphon → <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/">drain guide</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>E25</strong></td><td><strong>Drain pump blocked, or pump cover loose/missing</strong></td><td>Debris in impeller / reseat cover → <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/">drain guide</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Service codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Technician diagnostic codes (E01–E09) — not in your manual.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Real codes, used in Bosch service literature, but not published on Bosch's customer self-help site. If you're seeing one of these, it's internal hardware.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Part</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>E01</td><td>Control board / heating-relay fault</td><td>Main control board</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E02</td><td>NTC temperature sensor fault</td><td>Temperature sensor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E04</td><td>Flow-meter / water-flow signal (service analog of E14)</td><td>Flow meter / inlet path</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E05</td><td>Overfill / too much water</td><td>Float switch or inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E06</td><td>Door switch / door-lock fault</td><td>Door latch assembly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E09</td><td>Heating element / heater circuit fault</td><td>Heating element or board</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">E15 — the most common one</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Leak detected. Drain the pan, then find the leak.</h2>
    <p>E15 is Bosch's most-seen service code. The AquaStop float in the base pan sensed water and shut off the intake. Clearing it is two steps: tip the machine back over towels so the pan water pours out the front (the pan has no drain), which drops the float, then cut power for about 5 minutes to hard-reset. But E15 will return unless the underlying leak — door seal, inlet valve, a hose, or the sump — gets found and fixed, so the reset alone is only half the job.</p>
    <p>Because E15 is Bosch's number-one call and has its own diagnostic path, we keep the full guide here: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-e15-error/">Bosch dishwasher E15 error</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Drain codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">E22, E24, E25 — related, but different parts.</h2>
    <p>The drain-cluster codes overlap, so here's the clean split: <strong>E22</strong> is filter-blocked / residual water in the sump (clean the filter cylinder first). <strong>E24</strong> is a blocked drain route — filter plus drain hose and siphon connection. <strong>E25</strong> is the drain pump itself — debris jamming the impeller, or a loose/missing pump cover. All three start at the filter, but E25 specifically means "check the pump and its cover."</p>
    <p>Rather than repeat the whole step-by-step here, the full Bosch drain walkthrough (filter, hose, siphon, pump, cover) lives on <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/">Bosch dishwasher not draining</a> — that page owns the drain fix; this one owns the code lookup.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Fill codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">E14, E16, E17, E18 — water in and flow sensing.</h2>
    <p><strong>E18 (low fill):</strong> not enough water drawn in. Open the tap fully, straighten the supply hose, clean the AquaStop and inlet filters — Bosch wants about 10 liters/minute from a fully open tap, so weak household pressure alone can trip it. <strong>E16 / E17 (over-inflow):</strong> the opposite — water coming in when it shouldn't, an inlet valve not closing; check hose kinks and the supply first. <strong>E14 (flow meter):</strong> the flow-sensing Reed switch — a technician diagnosis. Inlet-side repairs run $200 to $380.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Cut power at the breaker (or unplug) for 5 minutes, restore, run a short cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>E15 — drain the pan.</strong> Tip the machine back over towels so base-pan water pours out, then power-cycle. Fix the leak source or it returns.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the filter (E22 / E24).</strong> Twist out the filter cylinder at the tub bottom, rinse, reinstall.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the drain path (E24 / E25).</strong> Drain hose kinks, siphon connection, pump cover seated.</li>
      <li><strong>Open the tap (E18).</strong> Supply valve fully open, hose straight, inlet filters clear.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Service codes (E01–E09), flow meter (E14), a jammed drain pump (E25), and a returning E15 mean a real fault — book a visit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Bosch is worth repairing longer than most.</h2>
    <p>Bosch and Thermador dishwashers are built to a durable standard, and their parts catalog stays stable, so a single fault — a leak, a drain blockage, a door latch — is almost always worth fixing rather than replacing, even on an older machine. The place we'll tell you to think twice is a control-board failure (E01) on a unit that's also showing other faults, where the board cost approaches a new mid-range machine. We give you that math at the door. $89 diagnostic, waived if you go ahead with the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
